First things first: if you want to be succesful at NetHack, you'll have
to learn where the shift key on your keyboard is. Finding the apostrophe
could also be helpful.

> Food rations are safe, don't bother pet testing...  If you find an 
> altar, test them for cursed status...Cursed has less food value. 

Don't bother testing food rations, or any other kind of man-made food.
All food is created uncursed. There are situations in which you can find
cursed food, but for a beginner, they're not that relevant. The one
exception to this: tins. They _can_ be cursed, and cursed tins _are_
bad. The risk is not that great, but you might want to check.

> The note about pets eating applied to classes of corpses, if your dog eats a 
> gnome, you can eat gnomes, Pets won't eat vegetables, so the lichen 
> wasn't appealing, yes, you can eat it.

Note: carnivore pets (cats and dogs, to beginners) don't eat veggies.
Ponies won't eat animal corpses.

Note also: poisoning is not the same thing as food poisoning. Poisoning
comes from eating some kinds of food (certain corpses, mainly) and can
be avoided by not eating corpses your pets won't eat. Food poisoning
comes from eating over-ripe corpses (including those that would be safe
if fresh), and can be avoided by only eating fresh corpses. Neither of
those apply to man-made food.

Note the third: some food (corpses, and other food) can have other
effects than nutrition, and this can be good or bad.

